@page "/docs/accessibility/automated-and-manual"

@* The line between what a tool settles, what it flags for a person, and what only a person checks: for izzy audit
   (Audit.cs, AuditSweeps.cs, the reports' "Not checked" list) and for any other tool. Israel, Nov 25, 2026: "accessibility
   can be sensitive, so we need to make sure we are clear where the line is drawn about what can be automated vs what
   will need human intervention and interaction". Claims here are about checks, never about conformance. *@

<ArticlePage Section="accessibility" Slug="automated-and-manual">
    <Lead>
        A tool measures a page. It doesn't use one. Some of what WCAG asks is a measurement, and a tool
        settles it. Some is a judgment, where a tool finds the place and a person decides. The rest shows up
        only when a person uses the page with a keyboard and a screen reader. The line is the same for
        <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit">izzy audit</Anchor> and for any tool like it.
    </Lead>
    <ChildContent>
        <Heading Level="2">Three kinds of check</Heading>
        <Table Items="Kinds" Caption="Who settles a check" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Kind" Width="Length.Rem(12)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Who settles it">@context!.Who</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="In izzy audit">@context!.Audit</TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>

        <Heading Level="2">What a tool settles</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            These are measurements. A tool's report of one is usually right, and
            <Code>izzy audit</Code>'s verdict says when a rule is wrong about a pattern. A fix can be checked by
            running the tool again. No finding means the measurement passed on what the tool could measure, as
            the page loaded.
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>A control, picture or frame has no name at all.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Text on a plain background has less contrast than 4.5:1, or 3:1 for large text.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>ARIA is wrong: a role that doesn't exist, an attribute its role doesn't allow, a required child that is missing.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>The page has no <Code>lang</Code> or no title.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A heading skips a level. This is a best practice, not a WCAG criterion.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A control is smaller than 24 by 24 pixels, takes clicks in a smaller area and has no room around it. Whether an exception applies, such as a link in a sentence, is for a person.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>A link or button that takes focus stays hidden under a header or footer that stays in place. A pass covers only that case.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>In forced colors, a control doesn't change at all when it takes focus. WCAG doesn't name forced colors: this check finds what people who use a contrast theme lose.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">What a tool points at, and a person decides</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            A tool finds the place. A person decides. <Code>izzy audit</Code> never counts these as passed. It
            reports them in one of two ways:
        </TextBlock>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>A finding with the verdict needs a person: izzy measured something a person has to judge. <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/audit-verdicts#needs-a-person">Verdicts</Anchor> lists the cases.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>An element counted apart as not measured: text over a picture or a gradient, an id that two elements share, or an <Code>aria-labelledby</Code> that points to an id nothing has.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">What only a person can check</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            No tool settles these. Where a tool checks a small part, such as whether a video has a captions
            track, a clean result says little. A report with no findings says nothing about them.
        </TextBlock>
        <Table Items="Manual" Caption="Checks that need a person" Size="ControlSize.Sm" Class="site-params site-params-prose" Bordered="false">
            <Columns>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Check" Width="Length.Rem(14)">@context!.Name</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="Why a tool can't">@context!.Why</TemplateColumn>
                <TemplateColumn Title="How to check it"><Anchor Href="@($"docs/accessibility/testing#{context!.Anchor}")">@context.Section</Anchor></TemplateColumn>
            </Columns>
        </Table>
        <TextBlock>
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/testing">Testing your app</Anchor> has the steps for each check.
        </TextBlock>

        <Heading Level="2">What a report can claim</Heading>
        <List Gap="2">
            <ListItem>A finding with the verdict real is one izzy has no reason to excuse. Treat it as a failure of the criterion its rule names, and fix it. A few rules are best practices with no criterion, and the report says so.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>No findings means the rules that ran found nothing, on the pages that were audited, in the state they loaded in. It doesn't mean the page meets WCAG.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>Conformance is a claim a person makes for whole pages and whole tasks, after testing every criterion by tool and by hand. An Accessibility Conformance Report (a filled-in VPAT) or an accessibility statement needs that work. An audit report is one input.</ListItem>
            <ListItem>An accepted finding is your decision, with your reason. It is not a pass, accepting it doesn't make a failure conform, and the report keeps showing it.</ListItem>
        </List>

        <Heading Level="2">What IzzyBlazor claims for itself</Heading>
        <TextBlock>
            The same line holds for the library. Its components are built to WCAG 2.2 level AA, and the
            <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/overview">overview</Anchor> says how that is checked and which gaps
            are known. <Anchor Href="docs/accessibility/component-status">Component status</Anchor> lists, for each
            component, what a tool checked and which screen readers it has been run through. Where a check hasn't
            been done, it says so.
        </TextBlock>
        <TextBlock>
            Accessible components don't make an app conform. The names, the headings, the order of the page and
            the words in an error are yours.
        </TextBlock>
    </ChildContent>
</ArticlePage>

@code {
    private sealed record Row(string Name, string Who, string Audit);

    private static readonly Row[] Kinds =
    [
        new("A measurement", "The tool. The answer is a number or a fact about the markup.", "A finding with a verdict, or no finding."),
        new("A judgment a tool can point at", "A person, looking at the place the tool found.", "A finding with the verdict needs a person, or an element counted as not measured."),
        new("Use of the page", "A person with a keyboard, a screen reader, zoom and a contrast theme.", "Not checked. report.md and report.html list it under what the run did not check."),
    ];

    private sealed record Check(string Name, string Why, string Section, string Anchor);

    // Section and Anchor are a heading of Testing.razor, where the steps are: keep them in step with it.
    private static readonly Check[] Manual =
    [
        new("Names that make sense", "A tool sees that a button has a name, not whether \"Delete\" says which invoice.", "With a screen reader", "with-a-screen-reader"),
        new("Alt text that fits", "A tool sees that a picture has alt text, not whether it describes the picture.", "With a screen reader", "with-a-screen-reader"),
        new("Keyboard order and traps", "The order is right or wrong against the page's meaning.", "With a keyboard", "with-a-keyboard"),
        new("Every feature by keyboard", "A tool doesn't know what your page is for.", "With a keyboard", "with-a-keyboard"),
        new("Focus you can see", "A tool can count the pixels that change, not whether you notice them.", "With a keyboard", "with-a-keyboard"),
        new("Dialogs, menus and other states", "An audit sees the page as it loads. A tool can check an open dialog only if you open it first.", "With a keyboard", "with-a-keyboard"),
        new("Errors and instructions", "Whether a message tells the user what to do is a matter of words.", "With a keyboard", "with-a-keyboard"),
        new("What a screen reader says", "Browsers and screen readers differ, and timing matters.", "With a screen reader", "with-a-screen-reader"),
        new("Announcements", "A tool sees a live region, not whether it speaks at the right moment.", "With a screen reader", "with-a-screen-reader"),
        new("Structure and reading order", "A tool sees headings, lists and tables, not whether they match the page.", "With a screen reader", "with-a-screen-reader"),
        new("Content on hover", "A tooltip or popover isn't there as the page loads.", "With a pointer", "with-a-pointer"),
        new("Drags and gestures", "A tool can't tell that a drag has another way.", "With a pointer", "with-a-pointer"),
        new("Zoom and reflow", "Content that is cut off at 200% still measures as present.", "Zoom, contrast and motion", "zoom-contrast-and-motion"),
        new("A real contrast theme", "A browser's forced colors show what disappears, not a person's own colors.", "Zoom, contrast and motion", "zoom-contrast-and-motion"),
        new("Contrast of icons and borders", "Tools measure the contrast of text.", "Zoom, contrast and motion", "zoom-contrast-and-motion"),
        new("Meaning by color alone", "A tool doesn't know what a color means.", "Zoom, contrast and motion", "zoom-contrast-and-motion"),
        new("Time limits, motion and media", "A tool doesn't wait for a session to end or watch a video.", "Zoom, contrast and motion", "zoom-contrast-and-motion"),
    ];
}
